module gunbc.process_lifecycle

// ONE BOUNDED WAIT ON A PROCESS THIS LAYER STARTED.
//
// WHAT IS SHARED HERE, AND WHY IT IS NOT IN extdeps. The syscall layer's contribution is waitpid(2) with
// WNOHANG, and that is already modelled, whole, in #extdeps.posix.process::Reap: StillRunning, Exited with
// the raw code, Signaled with the fatal signal, or ReapRefused when waitpid itself answers ECHILD or an
// error. Nothing in POSIX adds a poll cadence, a pause between polls, or a duration at which the caller
// gives up. Those three are a DECISION of the layer that imposed a bound on a process it started: the
// one-second cadence, the pause, and the countdown are runtime policy, and policy that lives in extdeps
// stops being auditable -- a reader of extdeps.posix.process could no longer tell which facts may be
// trusted as the upstream's and which are ours (gunbc.design_argument::external_upstreams: extdeps carries
// UPSTREAM facts). So the waitpid answer stays in extdeps and the bounded wait over it lives here, in
// gunbc, beside the two consumers and above neither of them.
//
// THE RULE, ONCE. Poll Reap; the first answer that is not StillRunning is returned unchanged; while the
// answer is StillRunning, pause one second and count the remaining bound down; when the bound runs out with
// the subject still alive the answer is StillRunning, which is NOT a fact about the subject -- it is the
// signal that the CALLING layer must decide what a survivor means. Every consumer therefore sees one
// cadence, one countdown arithmetic, and one exhaustion answer, and none of them gets to invent its own.
//
// WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS AT ALL (gunbc.design_argument::root_generalization). The trigger is the second
// local implementation of one rule, and two existed: gunbc.harness.harness_shell_job::harness_await_pid and
// gunbc.runner.runner_microvm_boot_probe's boot_probe_await_reap were the same 18 lines bar one widening.
// Both leaves are DELETED here -- neither module keeps a copy, and nothing re-exports this under a local
// name, because a generalization beside surviving leaves is a second path. The two consumers over executing
// routes: harness_shell_job's job backstop (harness_kill_running_job confirms a group kill by reaping) and
// the boot probe's bounded invocation (boot_probe_after_first_reap and boot_probe_confirm_signal). Their
// witnesses spawn real processes, so the sharing is exercised, not asserted:
// test.claim.harness_shell_job_process_wet_witness and
// test.claim.runner.runner_microvm_boot_probe_lifecycle_witness.
//
// NEAR COPIES DELIBERATELY LEFT ALONE, named rather than silently kept.
//   gunbc.instruments.emit_host_transport::node_http_await_reaped polls the same Reap on a 0.1s cadence,
//   bounded by an Int attempt count rather than a Second, and answers `none` at exhaustion instead of
//   StillRunning. That is a different contract on a different subject (a smoke server this instrument
//   started), owned by another node; folding it in would change a smoke gate's observable answer under this
//   node's cover. It is named here so the next author finds the rule already modelled instead of writing a
//   third one.
//   The SIGTERM -> grace -> SIGKILL ladder is NOT here either, and is not a second implementation of THIS
//   rule: it is one implementation of a different rule, with one consumer today
//   (gunbc.runner.runner_microvm_boot_probe: boot_probe_enforce_deadline, boot_probe_after_group_signal,
//   boot_probe_confirm_signal, boot_probe_escalate_kill). Its census: `posix.Signal.KillProcessGroup` has
//   exactly one CODE call site in the tree (that probe), and harness's kill path is a single-shot TERM with
//   no grace and no escalation. root_generalization's own trigger -- a SECOND local implementation -- has
//   not fired for it, so moving it now would be a generalization in search of a consumer. The follow-up
//   that consumes a bounder beyond its first site is the landed roadmap row
//   bare-124-timeout-readers-consume-the-bound-observation (dispatchable, owner fleet), which is where this
//   ladder's second home belongs.
//
// WHAT A CONSUMER MUST NOT DO WITH THE ANSWER. A StillRunning from here is not evidence about the subject
// and must never be rendered as a fired deadline by the layer that receives it: only the layer that
// IMPOSED the bound may say it fired, and it says so from its own decision to signal, not from a status
// (gunbc.design_argument::epistemic_humility, and the failure-mode row
// bare_exit_124_read_as_an_observed_timeout_event).

import std.types { Int, NonEmptyStr }
import extdeps.posix.process
import extdeps.tools.sleep
import std.measure { Second, second, measure_le, measure_sub, MeasureDifference, MeasureSubtrahendExceedsMinuend }

// The one-second reap poll described above. `remaining` is the bound the CALLER still holds; the recursion
// is bounded by it, and both the zero and the underflow answers are StillRunning, so no caller can be left
// without an answer and none can be handed a fabricated death.
fn process_await_reap(pid: Int, remaining: Second) -> ProcessReapOutcome {
  let reaped = posix.Process.Reap(pid: pid)
  match reaped.reaped {
    StillRunning =>
      if measure_le(a: remaining, b: second(count: 0)) {
        StillRunning
      } else {
        let _pause = sleep.Delay.Seconds(seconds: "1" as NonEmptyStr)
        match measure_sub(a: remaining, b: second(count: 1)) {
          MeasureDifference { value: next } => process_await_reap(pid: pid, remaining: next)
          MeasureSubtrahendExceedsMinuend => StillRunning
        }
      }
    Exited { code } => Exited { code: code }
    Signaled { signal } => Signaled { signal: signal }
    ReapRefused { cause } => ReapRefused { cause: cause }
  }
}
